{
  "teamId": 142,
  "year": 2019,
  "narrative": "The 2019 Minnesota Twins announced themselves to the baseball world early and emphatically. After a modest but promising March, the team caught fire in April and absolutely ignited in May, posting a jaw-dropping 21-8 record that month and establishing themselves as genuine American League contenders. Built around a historic home run attack — they would finish with a then-record 307 long balls — the Bomba Squad played with an offensive freedom that made Target Field electric all summer long. Eddie Rosario, Max Kepler, Mitch Garver, Miguel Sanó, Nelson Cruz, and Byron Buxton all contributed signature moments to a lineup that punished opponents with relentless power.\n\nThe middle months tested the Twins in ways the box scores only partially capture. June brought a 15-12 record that masked the brilliance of individual moments — Buxton's go-ahead blast, Kepler's clutch shot, Cruz's walk-off double in Tampa — while July's 13-11 mark came packaged with wild swings of fortune. A particularly turbulent stretch against Oakland in late July saw Rogers surrender a devastating walk-off home run to Aaron Hicks, and a roller-coaster series that included Adrianza's unlikely heroics and Sanó's booming answers. The team was never in danger of losing the division race, but those midsummer battles sharpened their character.\n\nAugust and September saw the Twins pull away convincingly, going 17-11 and then 18-9 to finish at 101 wins — their best record in franchise history since the 2009 squad. Marwin Gonzalez provided clutch multi-run home runs, Sano kept mashing, and the rotation steadied. The team clinched the AL Central and rightly celebrated one of the most offensively dominant seasons in franchise history. They had rewritten the record books for home runs and proven that a small-market club could build a genuine powerhouse.\n\nOctober, however, delivered a familiar and heartbreaking verdict. Matched against the New York Yankees in the ALDS, the Twins showed flashes — Polanco homered, Cruz connected, Sanó went deep — but the Yankees' relentless lineup proved too much. Berríos and the bullpen could not hold leads, Gleyber Torres hurt them twice, and the Bomba Squad's bats fell quiet when the margin for error shrank to zero. The dynasty that felt inevitable in May ended in the first round, leaving Minnesota to wonder what might have been — and hungry to return.",
  "chapters": [
